 ## Overview
-This method computes the gradient of the loss function $L$ with respect to the opacity parameter $\alpha_k$ of the $k$-th Gaussian in Time-of-Flight (ToF) image formation models, specifically for phasor or quad pixel signals. It extends the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) color rendering gradient by introducing squared transmittance terms in the recursive accumulation, addressing the non-linear interaction of opacity in ToF signal integration. It solves the problem of back-propagating errors through the ToF forward rendering equation, which differs from the linear alpha-blending used in standard RGB rendering.
+This method computes the gradient of the loss function with respect to the opacity parameter $\alpha_k$ of the $k$-th Gaussian for Time-of-Flight (ToF) image formation models, specifically for phasor or quad pixel signals [S1]. It extends the standard differentiable alpha-compositing gradient used in RGB rendering to handle the non-linear accumulation of ToF signals, where the rendered signal depends on the squared transmittance of occluding Gaussians [S1]. Unlike standard RGB rendering which uses a linear accumulation of color contributions, ToF rendering requires a recursive accumulator that weights later contributions by the square of their transmittance, making the gradient computation less trivial [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** The loss function $L$, the rendered ToF signal $\mathbf{q}(\mathbf{x})$ (e.g., quad pixels) at pixel $\mathbf{x}$, the opacity $\alpha_k$, the quad contribution $\mathbf{q}_k$ of the $k$-th Gaussian, the transmittance $T_k$, and the accumulated quad signal $\text{acc}_k^q$ from subsequent Gaussians.
-*   **Output:** The gradient $\frac{\partial L}{\partial \alpha_k}$.
+*   **Input:** The loss gradient with respect to the rendered ToF signal $\frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{s}(\mathbf{x})}$ (where $\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{x})$ is the signal, e.g., quad pixel $q(\mathbf{x})$ or phasor $p(\mathbf{x})$), the transmittance $T_k(\mathbf{x})$, the opacity $\alpha_k(\mathbf{x})$, and the per-Gaussian signal contribution $\mathbf{s}_k(\mathbf{x})$ (e.g., $\mathbf{q}_k$).
+*   **Output:** The gradient of the loss with respect to the opacity parameter $\frac{\partial L}{\partial \alpha_k}$.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   $\mathbf{q}_k$: A vector representing the quad pixel contribution of the $k$-th Gaussian. For standard quad pixels, this typically contains 4 components (e.g., phase shifts or intensity samples).
-*   $\text{acc}_k^q$: An accumulator variable of the same dimension as $\mathbf{q}_k$, storing the aggregated contribution of Gaussians $k+1$ to $N$.
-*   $T_k$: Scalar transmittance up to Gaussian $k$.
-*   $\alpha_k$: Scalar opacity of Gaussian $k$.
+The method relies on a single scalar accumulator variable, $\text{acc}_k$, which stores the weighted sum of signal contributions from all Gaussians deeper than $k$.
 
